 \"has_titles\": false,\n  1421→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1422→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1423→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1424→    },\n  1425→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1426→      \"plot_summary\": \"The story begins in the midst of a terrifying pandemic known as the Red Death, a merciless plague characterized by bleeding from the pores and swift, agonizing death. Fleeing the devastation, Prince Prospero retreats with a thousand courtiers into a secluded, heavily fortified abbey, sealing themselves off from the outside world in a desperate bid for safety and indulgence. Within the abbey, he designs seven opulent, themed rooms, each adorned with unique stained-glass windows and lavish decor, culminating in a final, ominous black chamber with blood-red panes and a towering ebony clock that chimes each hour, freezing the revelry in dread. The atmosphere is one of decadence and denial, as the elite attempt to escape reality through extravagant masked balls and hedonistic pleasures.\\n\\nAs the festivities reach their peak during a grand masked ball, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in grave-cloths and stained with vivid red, mimicking the plague’s gruesome symptoms. The guests are paralyzed with terror, their revelry shattered by the grotesque intrusion. Prince Prospero, furious at the sacrilege and the disruption of his illusion of control, draws a dagger and pursues the figure through the seven rooms, each one symbolizing a stage of life and decay. His pursuit is both a physical and symbolic journey, reflecting his futile attempt to defy death and maintain his power and privilege.\\n\\nThe climax unfolds in the black chamber, where the figure is revealed to be an empty shell—no living person, but a manifestation of the very plague they sought to escape. Prince Prospero collapses and dies instantly, his arrogance and denial finally undone. In that moment, the Red Death manifests among the revelers: one by one, they fall, their bodies succumbing to the same symptoms that ravaged the world outside. The lights go out, the music ceases, and silence descends as the abbey is claimed by death. The story ends with the complete annihilation of the isolated elite, underscoring the inevitability of mortality and the futility of attempting to escape it through wealth, power, or isolation.\",\n  1427→      \"themes\": [\n  1428→        \"identity\",\n  1429→        \"ambition\",\n  1430→        \"loss\"\n  1431→      ],\n  1432→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1433→    },\n  1434→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1435→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1436→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1437→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1438→      },\n  1439→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1440→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1441→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1442→      },\n  1443→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1444→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1445→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1446→      },\n  1447→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n  1448→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1449→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1450→      },\n  1451→      \"Pronunciation Guide\": {\n  1452→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1453→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1454→      }\n  1455→    },\n  1456→    \"timing\": {\n  1457→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1458→        \"duration_seconds\": 15.52,\n  1459→        \"duration_formatted\": \"15.5s\"\n  1460→      },\n  1461→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1462→        \"duration_seconds\": 288.58,\n  1463→        \"duration_formatted\": \"4m 49s\"\n  1464→      },\n  1465→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1466→        \"duration_seconds\": 26.55,\n  1467→        \"duration_formatted\": \"26.6s\"\n  1468→      },\n  1469→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n 